# Cyrius Field Notes — Language Gotchas (parser & syntax)
# Operator precedence, var/scope/bracket parser rules, and shift semantics that
# bite at parse time or at the lexer.
# 12 entries.

[[entries]]
name = "the_logical_shift_trap"
description = "Cyrius >> is LOGICAL not arithmetic — the bug that made every wall invisible"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Update (v6.4.46)**: Cyrius now ships a NATIVE arithmetic
(sign-preserving) right-shift operator `>>>`, superseding the
hand-rolled `asr()` helper below — write `a >>> 16` directly.
`>>` is STILL a LOGICAL shift (zero-fill). Cyrius DELIBERATELY
REVERSES the JS/Java convention: in JS/Java `>>` = arithmetic and
`>>>` = logical; in Cyrius `>>` = logical and `>>>` = arithmetic.
The DOOM debugging story + the `asr()` workaround are preserved
below for context (and for any pre-v6.4.46 toolchain).

---

THE BUG THAT BLOCKED RENDERING FOR HOURS:

After building the entire DOOM engine — WAD parser, BSP traversal, column
renderer, 320x200 framebuffer — the output was a black screen. 0 pixels
drawn. 0 columns clipped. The renderer was running but producing nothing.

THE ROOT CAUSE: Cyrius >> is a LOGICAL right shift (zero-fills upper bits).
Not ARITHMETIC (sign-extends). This is different from C's >> on signed types.

Every fixed-point operation that produced a negative intermediate was broken:

  fixed_mul(a, b) = (a * b) >> 16

  If a=100 and b=-50 (both in 16.16): a*b = negative.
  Logical >> 16 zero-fills the top, producing a HUGE POSITIVE number.
  The projection math then puts every wall at screen Y = +billions.

This affected:
  - fixed_mul: every negative product
  - fixed_to_int: every negative coordinate
  - fixed_round: every negative value
  - point_on_side: every point with negative dx or dy
  - Every projection calculation involving negative coordinates

DOOM E1M1 player start is at (1056, -3616). The negative Y meant that
EVERY calculation involving the player position was garbage.

THE FIX: An arithmetic-shift-right helper:

  fn asr(val, bits) {
      if (val >= 0) { return val >> bits; }
      var pos = 0 - val;
      return 0 - (pos >> bits);
  }

Replace every >> with asr() for signed values:
  fn fixed_mul(a, b) { return asr(a * b, 16); }
  fn fixed_to_int(f) { return asr(f, 16); }

MODERN FIX (v6.4.46+): drop asr() entirely — use the native `>>>`
arithmetic-shift operator, which sign-extends:
  fn fixed_mul(a, b) { return (a * b) >>> 16; }
  fn fixed_to_int(f) { return f >>> 16; }

RESULT: 46,110 pixels drawn. 320/320 columns clipped. Walls visible.
The screenshot shows E1M1 from the player start position with correct
BSP-ordered walls, floor, and ceiling.

LESSON: In any language, check whether >> is arithmetic or logical
BEFORE writing fixed-point math. In C, >> on signed types is
implementation-defined (but usually arithmetic on x86). In Cyrius,
it is definitively logical. In Java, >>> is logical and >> is
arithmetic. In Rust, >> is arithmetic for signed types.

This one operator difference was the difference between a working
DOOM renderer and a black screen.

DETECTION: The fuzz harness did NOT catch this because it only checked
for crashes (SIGFPE, segfault), not for incorrect values. A property-
based test like "fixed_to_int(fixed_from_int(n)) == n for negative n"
would have caught it instantly. Add algebraic property tests to your
fuzz harnesses, not just crash tests.

[[entries]]
name = "no_var_redecl_same_scope"
description = "✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.8.x — `var` re-declaration in sibling if/elif/else branches now compiles cleanly; PRE-FIX behavior + workarounds preserved below for pre-v5.8.x toolchain users"
category = "cyrius"
---
✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.8.x. Empirical premise-check at the v5.8.47
vidya audit boundary: `if (x == 1) { var buf[8]; } elif (y == 1) {
var buf[16]; }` compiles cleanly + runs correctly. The fix landed
as a side-effect of the v5.8.x scope/local-table work (exact
shipping version not bisected — same pattern as the v5.8.46
arithmetic-in-fn-args entry, where the fix landed earlier in the
cycle as a side-effect of expression-grammar work and the entry
went stale waiting for an explicit refresh).

PRE-v5.8.x BEHAVIOR (kept for historical context + workaround
text for anyone still on a pre-v5.8.x toolchain):

Cyrius treats all branches of an if / elif / else chain as ONE
scope for `var` purposes. Declaring the same name in two branches
is a compile error ("duplicate variable"), even though the two
bodies never execute together.

    if (x) {
        var buf[8];
        # ...
    } elif (y) {
        var buf[16];    # ERROR: duplicate variable 'buf'
        # ...
    }

Same rule applies to do-something-then-do-something-similar idioms
where both arms declare a local named the same thing.

WORKAROUNDS:
1. Rename one: `buf_a` + `buf_b`.
2. Hoist the declaration above the if:
       var buf[16];
       if (x) { # use first 8 bytes }
       elif (y) { # use all 16 bytes }
3. Wrap each body in a bare `{ }` scope:
       if (x) { { var buf[8]; ... } }
       elif (y) { { var buf[16]; ... } }
   Bare braces open a fresh scope that the branch's `var` lives
   in, separate from the sibling branch.

WHY IT HAPPENS: cyrius's local-variable table is flat per
function, keyed by name. The scope-depth counter tracks lexical
depth for `#ifdef`-style gating but doesn't reset across sibling
branches. `var x` appends a new entry + asserts name uniqueness
over the whole function frame.

Surfaced v5.4.17 writing `lib/toml.cyr`'s multi-line array fix —
the array-scan branch and the unquoted-value fallback both
wanted a `var vstart`. Fixed by renaming the new branch's
local to `astart`.

[[entries]]
name = "no_comparisons_in_fn_args"
description = "✅ FIXED in v5.8.46 — comparisons + arithmetic now parse cleanly in fn-call argument positions"
category = "cyrius"
---
✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.8.46 (regression-floor lock). All six
comparison forms (`==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`, `>=`) AND all ten
binary arithmetic operators (`+ - * / % | & ^ << >>`) now parse
cleanly in function-call argument positions. Short-circuit `&&` /
`||` also fine. The originally-rejected shapes all work:

    foo(x == y, z);              # OK
    assert_eq(n > 0, 1, "name"); # OK
    biquad_set(a, 0, 0, a - K, 0); # OK (companion arithmetic case)

Closed via the v5.8.46 slot's regression-floor lock at
`tests/tcyr/expr_in_fn_args.tcyr` (30 assertions covering all 18
operator shapes × operand-source mix × arg-position variation,
plus the original `vidya/content/audio_dsp/cyrius.cyr`
`biquad_lowpass_1pole` repro). The actual fix landed earlier in
the v5.8.x cycle as a side-effect of the parser/expression-
grammar work — `lib/assert.cyr:11` already comment-noted "Now
that comparisons work in function arguments, assert(x == 42,
'name') works." — but neither this field-note nor the
`docs/development/issues/2026-05-03-arithmetic-in-fn-args-rejected.md`
issue file was refreshed until v5.8.46. Workaround text below
preserved for pre-v5.8.46 toolchain users.

PRE-v5.8.46 BEHAVIOR (kept for historical context):

Comparison operators return 0/1 and can live anywhere an integer
expression does — EXCEPT inside a function call's argument list.
The parser rejected them there because of an ambiguity with the
generic-bracket syntax reserved for future use.

    # REJECTED (pre-v5.8.46):
    foo(x == y, z);
    assert_eq(n > 0, 1, "non-negative");

    # OK — assign to a temp first (still works post-fix):
    var eq = (x == y);
    foo(eq, z);
    var nn = (n > 0);
    assert_eq(nn, 1, "non-negative");

Applied to all six comparison forms (`==`, `!=`, `<`, `>`, `<=`,
`>=`). Short-circuit `&&` / `||` and arithmetic operators were
also (sometimes) rejected; the companion case was tracked as
arithmetic-in-fn-args (issue closed alongside).

Error message before the fix: the parser's generic hard-error
for unexpected-token-in-arg-list. No special diagnostic. The
v5.8.46 lock asserts the working forms; if the parser regresses,
the test catches it before ship.

[[entries]]
name = "caret_binds_tighter_than_binary_minus"
description = "`a ^ 0 - 1` is `(a ^ 0) - 1`, NOT `a ^ -1` — but NOT because `^` binds tighter. `^` and `-` are the SAME tier, left-associative. (Entry name is a misnomer kept for link stability; corrected at the v6.4.82 closeout, which also confirmed unary `~` DOES exist.)"
category = "cyrius"
---
**CORRECTED 2026-07-27 (v6.4.82 closeout).** The OBSERVED behaviour
below has always been right; the EXPLANATION was wrong, and one
"cyrius has no..." claim had gone stale. Both re-verified by running
the compiler:

  * `^` does NOT bind tighter than `-`. `^`, `|`, `&`, `+` and `-`
    all share ONE tier, evaluated LEFT to RIGHT. `x ^ 0 - 1` is
    `(x ^ 0) - 1` for the same reason `1 - 2 + 3` is `(1-2)+3`:
    equal precedence, left association. See
    `bitwise_ops_share_the_additive_tier` for the full tier table.
  * **Unary `~` EXISTS.** `~5 == -6`, probed at v6.4.82. The line
    below saying cyrius has no `~` is retired — `x ^ -1` still
    works and is still fine to read, but `~x` is the direct form.

---

The bite: writing the bitwise-NOT idiom as `value ^ 0 - 1` hoping to
get `value ^ -1`:

    var not_x = x ^ 0 - 1;       # parses as (x ^ 0) - 1 = x - 1  ← WRONG
    var not_x = x ^ (0 - 1);     # OK via parens
    var not_x = x ^ -1;          # OK — cyrius supports negative literals
    var not_x = ~x;              # OK — unary ~ (the direct form)

Surfaced v5.4.15 writing `lib/keccak.cyr`'s chi step. First
impl had `T1 ^ 0 - 1` and produced `2be83b...` for SHAKE128('')
instead of the NIST-canonical `7f9c2b...`. Changed to `T1 ^ -1`
and bytes matched FIPS 202 immediately.

GENERAL RULE when porting bitwise algorithms from C to cyrius:
- `~x` works; `x ^ -1` is the equivalent long form.
- `>>` on 64-bit ints is LOGICAL (verified via probe:
  `0x8000000000000001 >> 63 == 1`, not `-1`), so the naive
  left-rotate `(x << n) | (x >> (64 - n))` works without an
  explicit mask — as long as `n` is in `(0, 64)`. `>>>` (v6.4.46)
  is the arithmetic/sign-preserving one.
- Bitwise operands sit on the ADDITIVE tier, so ANY mixed
  bitwise+arithmetic expression ported from C needs parens
  re-checked, not just this one shape.

[[entries]]
name = "var_bracket_size_must_be_literal"
description = "`var name[N]` requires N to be a NUM literal OR (since v5.10.48) an enum-constant IDENT; plain `var NAME = LITERAL;` named-constant idents are still rejected. PARTIALLY FIXED v5.10.48."
category = "cyrius"
---
**Status (2026-07-04, v6.4.2)**: PARTIALLY FIXED at v5.10.48 — `var
name[N]` now accepts N as a NUM literal OR an IDENT that resolves to
a compile-time ENUM constant (parse_decl.cyr PARSE_ARRAY, via
enum_const_val marker bit 63). What is STILL rejected: an IDENT that
is a plain top-level `var NAME = LITERAL;` (cyrius has no
named-constant infrastructure outside enums). Pre-v5.10.48 behavior
+ workarounds preserved below. Tracked at:
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/2026-05-03-parser-cosmetic-limits-bare-return-and-var-bracket.md`.

The `var name[N]` stack-local syntax requires N to be a NUM literal
OR an enum-constant IDENT at parse time. A plain `var CONST = 32;`
name (not an enum constant) is still rejected.

    var TEX_STRUCT_SIZE = 32;

    fn build_texture() {
        var tex[TEX_STRUCT_SIZE];  # REJECTED at parse
        var tex[32];               # OK — literal
    }

This is consistent with the existing rule that `var name[N]` is
"N bytes of frame storage" (see `var_name_bracket_is_bytes_not_elements`).
The bytes are computed at parse, not at runtime, so the parser
needs the value before any name resolution runs.

WORKAROUNDS:

  1. Use a literal directly. If the value is shared between several
     declarations, write the literal in each spot and add a comment
     pointing to the documented size.
  2. Heap-allocate instead. `alloc(TEX_STRUCT_SIZE)` works fine —
     `alloc` takes a runtime expression, no parser constraint.
  3. Use an enum constant — this is now the CANONICAL fix (v5.10.48):
     `enum Sz { TEX = 32; } fn build_texture() { var tex[TEX]; }`.
     Enum constants are compile-time-resolved and accepted directly
     in the `var name[N]` position. (Only non-enum named `var`
     constants remain rejected.)

WHERE THIS BIT US:

mabda v3 Step 5.6 (native texture slot wrappers). Wanted

    var tex[NATIVE_TEX_STRUCT_SIZE];

with `NATIVE_TEX_STRUCT_SIZE = 32` defined elsewhere. Parser rejected
it. Used `var tex[32]` with a `# 32 = NATIVE_TEX_STRUCT_SIZE` comment.

The class of programs this affects: any helper that declares a
fixed-size frame buffer based on a structurally-meaningful constant
(struct size, packed-descriptor size, fixed-array stride). Until the
parser supports identifier-as-array-size, the constant has to be
duplicated as a literal at the call site.

[[entries]]
name = "multi_line_struct_enum_bodies_dont_parse"
description = "✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.8.x — multi-line struct + enum bodies parse cleanly; PRE-FIX behavior + single-line workaround preserved below for pre-v5.8.x toolchain users"
category = "cyrius"
---
✅ FIXED in cyrius v5.8.x. Empirical premise-check at the v5.8.47
vidya audit boundary: both
`struct Point { x: i64; y: i64; }` and the same shape spread
across multiple lines parse + compile cleanly; same for
multi-line `enum` bodies (`enum Color { Red = 1; Green = 2;
Blue = 3; }`). The fix landed as a side-effect of the v5.8.x
struct/enum work (sum-type syntax v5.8.21, exhaustive match
v5.8.22, the multi-slot enum-generics + dispatch run); exact
shipping version not bisected. Same stale-pin pattern as the
v5.8.46 arithmetic-in-fn-args entry.

PRE-v5.8.x BEHAVIOR (kept for historical context + single-line
workaround for anyone still on a pre-v5.8.x toolchain):

THE TRAP:

    struct Player {
        state;
        prev_state;
        timer;
    }

    enum PlayerState {
        PS_IDLE = 0;
        PS_RUN = 1;
        PS_SHOOT = 2;
    }

Cyrius's parser doesn't accept fields/variants on separate lines.
The visible error is misleading — usually "undefined variable
'TypeName'" pointing at an unrelated construct line, or
"unexpected ';'" in the middle of unrelated code far past the
real cause. The parser silently mis-tokenises the body and the
cascade manifests later.

CORRECT SHAPE — collapse the body onto one line:

    struct Player { state; prev_state; timer; }
    enum PlayerState { PS_IDLE = 0; PS_RUN = 1; PS_SHOOT = 2; }

WORKAROUND when the body would be unwieldy — split across
multiple struct/enum decls:

    enum PSNum1 { PS_IDLE = 0; PS_RUN = 1; PS_SHOOT = 2; PS_DUNK = 3; }
    enum PSNum2 { PS_PASS = 4; PS_STEAL = 5; PS_BLOCK = 6; PS_FALL = 7; }

WHERE THIS BIT US (v2.3.2 backfill sweep):
content/state_machines/cyrius.cyr (3 multi-line enums + 1 struct),
content/sprite_rendering/cyrius.cyr (1 multi-line struct),
content/game_ai_decisions/cyrius.cyr (1 multi-line enum + 1 struct).
All three failed to compile until bodies were collapsed. The
single-line form has been working since v3.x — see
content/projectile_physics/cyrius.cyr's `struct Ball { x; y; vx;
vy; }` for the canonical reference.

[[entries]]
name = "return_struct_literal_dangles_or_rejected"
description = "Returning a struct literal from a fn either fails to parse or returns a dangling stack pointer; construct in caller scope instead"
category = "cyrius"
---
THE TRAP — two failure modes, both wrong:

    # Mode 1 — parser rejects the brace after `return TypeName`:
    fn ball_new(x, y) {
        return Ball { x, y, 0, 0 };
    }
    # → error: undefined variable 'Ball' (misleading; actual cause
    #   is the parser disambiguating expression-vs-block at the brace)

    # Mode 2 — parses, but returns dangling stack memory:
    fn ball_new(x, y) {
        var b = Ball { x, y, 0, 0 };
        return &b;
    }
    # → silently corrupts: caller's frame use clobbers b's bytes,
    #   field reads return garbage. No compile error; only test
    #   asserts catch it (and only if the assertion is tight enough).

CORRECT SHAPE — construct in caller scope, pass `&local`:

    fn test_idle_to_run() {
        var p = Player { PS_IDLE, PS_IDLE, 0 };
        player_transition(&p, INPUT_MOVE);
        # &p is valid for the lifetime of test_idle_to_run's frame
    }

This is the convention every working topic in vidya uses
(content/projectile_physics/cyrius.cyr's Ball, post-v2.3.2 fix in
content/state_machines and content/game_ai_decisions). Builder
helpers like `ball_new` / `player_new` may stay in the file for
documentation but should be marked dead code — nothing calls
them.

If a long-lived heap-resident struct is genuinely needed, alloc()
the storage and write the fields explicitly:

    var b = alloc(BALL_SIZE);
    store64(b + BALL_X, x);
    store64(b + BALL_Y, y);
    return b;     # heap pointer, lives until process exit

WHERE THIS BIT US (v2.3.2):
content/game_ai_decisions/cyrius.cyr's `stats_new()` returned
&local_struct, silently passed garbage through to
ai_decide_offense, and the dunk test failed with "got 3, expected
1" (3 = ACT_DRIVE; the AI was reading `speed` from random stack
bytes that happened to outscore the dunk path). Fix: rewrite the
test to construct Stats inline.

[[entries]]
name = "bare_return_in_if_block_rejected"
description = "✅ FIXED in v5.10.48 — bare `return;` inside an `if { ... }` block now synthesizes `return 0;` (byte-identical codegen); pre-fix behavior + no-void-in-Cyrius note preserved below for pre-v5.10.48 toolchains"
category = "cyrius"
---
**Status (2026-07-04, v6.4.2)**: ✅ FIXED at v5.10.48. Bare
`return;` inside a braced block now synthesizes `return 0;` — the
parser peeks for `;` BEFORE calling PCMPE and, if present, emits
`mov rax, 0` + a jump to the epilogue (byte-identical to `return
0;` codegen at that position; parse_fn.cyr:685-699). The old
'unexpected ;' error no longer fires. Pre-fix behavior preserved
below for pre-v5.10.48 toolchain users. Tracked at:
`cyrius/docs/development/issues/2026-05-03-parser-cosmetic-limits-bare-return-and-var-bracket.md`.

PRE-v5.10.48 TRAP:

    fn fb_set(x, y, color) {
        if (x < 0) { return; }              # ERROR: unexpected ';'
        if (x >= SCREEN_W) { return; }
        ...
    }

Cyrius's parser fires "unexpected ';'" on bare `return;` inside
a braced block, even though bare `return;` at the top level of a
fn body is accepted. The reported error line number is unreliable
(preprocessed-line-number problem) — it points at some line dozens
or hundreds away from the real cause.

CORRECT SHAPE — return an explicit value. Cyrius has no void;
every fn returns i64:

    fn fb_set(x, y, color) {
        if (x < 0) { return 0; }
        if (x >= SCREEN_W) { return 0; }
        if (y < 0) { return 0; }
        if (y >= SCREEN_H) { return 0; }
        store8(_fb + y * SCREEN_W + x, color);
        return 0;
    }

WHY: the parser disambiguates `return EXPR;` vs block-statement
forms differently at fn-body top-level vs inside nested braces.
At top level, `return;` is accepted as a void shorthand; inside a
nested `{ ... }` the same form is rejected. The diagnostic doesn't
spell this out — it surfaces as a near-random "unexpected ';'"
wherever the post-error parser rebooted.

WHERE THIS BIT US (v2.3.2):
content/sprite_rendering/cyrius.cyr's fb_set + blit_scaled used
six `if (cond) { return; }` early-exits — all rejected. The
preprocessed line number reported was meaningless; bisecting the
file by truncating at function boundaries was the only way to
locate the root cause.

[[entries]]
name = "tagged_union_paren_consistency"
description = "in mixed enums, bare names stay int-typed and paren'd names heap-allocate — pick one shape per decl"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.8.21–v5.8.27 sum-type sub-suite shipped two distinct
variant shapes inside the SAME `enum` decl:

    enum Maybe {
        Nothing;       # bare name → int (auto-incremented from 0)
        Just(v);       # paren'd name → heap pointer (16-byte tagged)
    }

These two shapes do NOT compose at the value level:

    var n = Nothing;       # n = 0 (int)
    var j = Just(42);      # j = heap pointer
    if (n == j) { ... }    # comparing 0 to a heap address — never true

== CONVENTION ==

Pick one shape per enum decl. For sum types you'll
match against, use paren-consistent naming:

    enum Option { None(); Some(v); }      # both heap-allocated
    enum Result { Ok(v); Err(e); }
    enum Either { Left(v); Right(v); }

For pure int constants (mode flags, syscall numbers, errno),
use bare names:

    enum Color { RED; GREEN; BLUE; }
    enum SysNr { SYS_READ = 0; SYS_WRITE = 1; ... }
    enum KeyType { KeyTypeCstr; KeyTypeStr; KeyTypeU64; }

== WHY NOT AUTO-UNIFY ==

The v5.8.21 pin called for "bare-variant payload-shape
consistency" — making `Nothing` in a mixed-enum decl produce a
heap-allocated `tagged(N, 0)` automatically. v5.8.23 honest-
scope-shrunk this: empirical migration of `lib/tagged.cyr` used
paren-consistent shapes (`None()` / `Some(v)`) which work
cleanly without auto-tagging. Bare names stay int-typed; users
who want full tagged consistency add empty parens.

== EMPTY PAREN NULLARY (v5.8.23) ==

`Foo()` is a true arity-0 nullary tagged variant: 8-byte alloc,
tag at +0, no payload. Distinct from bare `Foo` (int constant)
and from `Foo(v)` (16-byte alloc, single payload).

== EXHAUSTIVE-MATCH CHECK SEMANTICS ==

The v5.8.22 + v5.8.25 coverage check fires only on enums that
have at least one PARSE_MATCH arm referencing a variant. Mixed
enums work — match on the int-named subset gets coverage'd
against the int values; match on the tagged subset against the
tagged values. But matching across both at once tends to confuse
intent — convention is to keep the modes separate.

[[entries]]
name = "match_on_tagged_value_needs_load64"
description = "match opt { Some => ... } compares heap pointers, not tags — use match load64(opt) for tag matching"
category = "cyrius"
---
v5.8.22 exhaustive-match coverage check fires correctly when
arms reference variants of an enum. But the runtime CMP at each
arm compares against the matchee VALUE — for tagged-union values
(heap pointers), that means comparing two heap pointers, never
equal at runtime.

== BROKEN ==

    var opt = Some(42);
    match opt {
        Some => { ... }     # Some folds to int 1 (tag)
                            # opt is a heap pointer
                            # cmp heap_ptr, 1 — never matches
        None => { ... }     # likewise
    }

== CORRECT ==

    var opt = Some(42);
    match load64(opt) {     # extract tag at +0 first
        Some => { ... }     # cmp 1, 1 — matches when opt is Some
        None => { ... }
    }

Or use the helper API from `lib/tagged.cyr` which encapsulates
the load64 pattern:

    if (is_some(opt) == 1) { var v = unwrap(opt); ... }
    var v = unwrap_or(opt, 0);

== WHY NOT AUTO-DEREF ==

Auto-deref on `match` would need:
  1. Type tracking — does the matchee evaluate to a tagged-union
     heap pointer or a plain int?
  2. Matchee cast — emit `mov rax, [rax]` to load tag before cmp.

Type tracking infrastructure isn't in the v5.8.x compiler yet
(monomorphization is post-v5.8.x). The exhaustive-match check
ships the metadata ergonomics; runtime match semantics use
the existing cmp/jcc-skip cascade unchanged.

Pattern destructuring (`match opt { Some(v) => use(v) }` binding
the payload) is also a future slot — needs both auto-deref AND
slot-binding from pattern offsets.

[[entries]]
name = "bitwise_ops_share_the_additive_tier"
description = "Cyrius precedence is NOT C's: `&`, `|`, `^` sit on the SAME tier as `+` and `-`, left-associative. `1 | 2 + 1` == 4 (C: 3). `5 & 3 + 1` == 2 (C: 4). Shifts are on the `*` tier. Comparisons are LOOSER than bitwise, so C's `x & MASK == 0` foot-gun does the right thing here."
category = "cyrius"
---
Every one of these was produced by RUNNING the compiler at the v6.4.82
closeout (`var a = <expr>; syscall(60, a);`), not read off a grammar.

THE TIERS (tightest first, each LEFT-associative):

    1  unary / factor    -x   ~x   &x   *p   f(...)   ( ... )
    2  TERM              *   /   %   <<   >>   >>>
    3  EXPR              +   -   &   |   ^        <-- ONE TIER
    4  comparison        ==  !=  <   >   <=  >=
    5  logical           &&  binds tighter than  ||   (split v6.3.36)

WHAT THAT COSTS YOU IF YOU THINK IN C:

    expression        cyrius   C      why
    ---------------   ------   ----   -------------------------------
    1 | 2 + 1         4        3      (1|2)+1   vs   1|(2+1)
    5 & 3 + 1         2        4      (5&3)+1   vs   5&(3+1)
    3 | 1 & 2         2        3      (3|1)&2   vs   3|(1&2)
    x ^ 0 - 1         x-1      x^-1   (x^0)-1   vs   x^(0-1)

Two that AGREE, so don't use them to test your understanding:
    1 & 2 | 3         3        3      C's & is tighter than its |,
                                      cyrius reads left-to-right —
                                      same answer, different reason
    2 + 3 & 1         1        1      C's + is tighter than its &,
                                      cyrius is left-to-right —
                                      again the same answer

WHAT IT BUYS YOU — the classic C mask bug is not a bug here:

    4 & 6 == 4        1        0      cyrius: (4&6)==4  → true
                                      C:      4&(6==4)  → 0
  In C you must write `(x & MASK) == 0`; in cyrius the unparenthesized
  form already means what you meant. Do not "fix" it back.

SHIFTS ARE TERM-TIER, not their own:

    1 + 2 << 1  == 5        # 1 + (2<<1)   — shift tighter than +
    6 >> 1 + 1  == 4        # (6>>1) + 1
    8 >> 1 * 2  == 8        # (8>>1)*2     — same tier as *, left-assoc
    2 * 3 >> 1  == 3        # (2*3)>>1
    8 >>> 1 + 1 == 5        # `>>>` is on the same tier as `>>`

PRACTICAL RULE: when porting ANY mixed arithmetic/bitwise expression
from C, Rust, Go, Python or JS, put the parens in. All four of those
languages give bitwise operators their own looser tiers; cyrius does
not. This is not a bug to be filed — it is the grammar. What IS worth
knowing is that it interacts badly with reading code aloud: `mask | base
+ offset` reads as "mask OR (base+offset)" to almost everyone and
computes `(mask|base)+offset`.

RELATED: `caret_binds_tighter_than_binary_minus` (the keccak bug this
tier layout caused, and the correction to its explanation).

[[entries]]
name = "float_bit_intrinsics_cannot_flank_term_operators"
description = "`a * f64_from(2)` and `f64_from(2) * a` are PARSE ERRORS — the f64_*/f32_*/f64v_*/iv_*/bitget/u128/ret2 intrinsics cannot appear on either side of `* / % << >> >>>`. `+ - & | ^` and comparisons are fine. Parenthesize or bind to a var. Confirmed live at v6.4.82."
category = "cyrius"
---
WHAT HAPPENS:

    var a: f64 = f64_from(84);
    var c: f64 = a / f64_from(2);
    error:<source>:2:18: unexpected f64_from
        var c: f64 = a / f64_from(2);
                         ^

    var c = f64_from(2) * a;
    error:<source>:2:21: expected ';', got '*'

Both directions fail. It is NOT float-specific — `var a = 3; var c = a
* f64_from(2);` fails identically with an untyped `a`.

THE AFFECTED SET (probed one by one at v6.4.82):

    FAILS as a TERM operand:  f64_from  f64_to  f64_add  f64_sqrt
                              f32_from  f32_to  (the whole f64_*/f32_*/
                              f64v_*/f32v_*/iv_* families)
                              bitget  bitset  bitclr
                              store8/16/32/64   u128   ret2 / rethi
    WORKS everywhere:         load8/16/32/64    syscall    sizeof
                              ordinary fn calls

THE AFFECTED OPERATORS:

    FAILS:  *   /   %   <<   >>   >>>          (the TERM tier)
    WORKS:  +   -   &   |   ^                  (the EXPR tier)
            ==  !=  <   >   <=  >=

WHY (for anyone reading the parser): these intrinsics are dispatched at
the HEAD of `PARSE_TERM` and each arm ends in `return 0`. So as a LEFT
operand the parse exits before reaching PARSE_TERM's `*`/`/` loop, and
as a RIGHT operand that loop calls `PARSE_FACTOR`, one tier DOWN, which
has never known these tokens. `+`/`-` work because `PEXPR`'s loop calls
`PARSE_TERM` — the tier that does know them.

WORKAROUNDS (both produce correct code today):

    var c: f64 = a * (f64_from(2));      # parens re-enter at the top
    var two: f64 = f64_from(2);          # or just bind it
    var c: f64 = a * two;

WHY THIS ENTRY EXISTS RATHER THAN A WORKAROUND RULE: this is a parser
gap, not a design choice, and per the repo's own standing rule a
codegen/parser defect must never be written down as a language rule for
users to code around. Treat the workaround as temporary. Found by
running the compiler during the v6.4.82 vidya closeout while checking
that the f32/f64 entry's examples actually compiled — the first draft
of that entry used `x / f64_from(2)` and did not.
